<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taranchi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taranchis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taranchi were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> farmers of the Ili valley in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, descended from people of the Tarim basin oases moved there by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> and later the Qing to grow grain. In 1864 they rose against the Qing and formed a sultanate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yining">Ghulja</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> occupied in 1871. Many moved to Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Semirechye</a> after the region was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1881.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taranchi.jpg" alt="Taranchi" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Lansde (1841-1919) or his travelling companions · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lansdell-1885-p231-The-Chief-Taranchi-Mosque-in-Kuldja.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Taranchi means simply a farmer in Chagatai, and the word could be used for the farming peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferghana-valley">Ferghana Valley</a> and of oases across Central Asian Turkestan. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> it named the Turkic-speaking, Muslim, settled people of the oases of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Kumul</a>, Khotan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a>, whose ancestors included Tocharians, Iranic peoples such as the Sakas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a>, and Mongolic tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>. The term stood against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarts">Sart</a>, the town traders and craftsmen, and against the ruling Moghol or Dolan nobility; these were classes in one culture rather than separate peoples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oases were ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> and then by the Naqshbandi Khojas, who split into Afaqi and Ishaqi factions. The Dzungar Khanate conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> by 1680 and set up the Afaqi Khojas as client rulers, taking heavy taxes in grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, labour and goods. After decades of war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a>, and Qing Manchu Bannermen carried out the Zunghar genocide, which nearly wiped them out and emptied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a>. China took full control of Dzungaria and the Tarim Basin by 1759.</p><p>The Qing military governors then encouraged Taranchis to settle the depopulated Ili valley beside Xibe garrisons and Kyrgyz nomads. About 10,000 families were moved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, and around 12,000 more families from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> after the invasion of Jahangir Khoja in the 1820s. During the Dungan revolt the Taranchi rose against the Qing; they first cooperated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dungan-people">Dungans</a> but then turned on them, massacring the Dungans at Kuldja.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the early 20th century, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> was divided among modern states, all the oasis farmers native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> nationality by 1934, while some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarts">Sarts</a> with strong ties to the west became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>. Standard Uyghur is based on the Taranchi dialect, which the Chinese government chose for that role.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taranchi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Erdeni Batur encouraged farming, and his successors settled thousands of Turkic-speaking Muslim farmers from the oases of the Tarim Basin in the Ili valley, where they grew grain for the nomads; their descendants became known as the <b>Taranchi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Ili valley, the <b>Taranchis</b>, Uyghur farmers descended from settlers brought by the Dzungars, joined the Dungans, captured the Qing garrisons of Ili, including Huiyuan, and established a sultanate at Ghulja.</blockquote><a href="/state/taranchi-sultanate">Taranchi Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
